---
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/Base.astro";

const sections = [
	{
		id: "preamble",
		number: "01",
		title: "Preamble",
		subtitle: "Why transformers changed everything",
	},
	{
		id: "architecture",
		number: "02",
		title: "Architecture Overview",
		subtitle: "The full picture, from input to output",
	},
	{
		id: "embeddings",
		number: "03",
		title: "Embeddings",
		subtitle: "Turning words into numbers",
	},
	{
		id: "attention",
		number: "04",
		title: "Attention",
		subtitle: "The mechanism that lets tokens talk to each other",
	},
	{
		id: "multi-head-attention",
		number: "05",
		title: "Multi-Head Attention",
		subtitle: "Many perspectives, one representation",
	},
	{
		id: "ffn",
		number: "06",
		title: "Feed-Forward Network",
		subtitle: "Per-token computation and memory",
	},
	{
		id: "residual-stream",
		number: "07",
		title: "Residual Stream",
		subtitle: "The information highway through the network",
	},
	{
		id: "output",
		number: "08",
		title: "Output",
		subtitle: "From vectors back to words",
	},
	{
		id: "circuits",
		number: "09",
		title: "Circuits",
		subtitle: "How attention heads compose into algorithms",
	},
];
---

<PageLayout meta={{ title: "How Transformers Work" }}>
	{/* Hero — full width, above the three-column layout */}
	<header class="hero mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pb-16 pt-8 text-center sm:px-6 sm:pt-16">
		<p class="mb-4 text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">
			Interactive Explainer
		</p>
		<h1 class="mb-6 text-4xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent-2 sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl">
			How Transformers Work
		</h1>
		<p class="mx-auto max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-textColor/80 sm:text-xl">
			A visual, interactive guide to the architecture behind modern AI — from
			tokens to predictions, attention to circuits.
		</p>
		<div class="mt-10 flex items-center justify-center gap-3 text-sm text-textColor/50">
			<span>Toby Lunt</span>
			<span class="text-accent/40">|</span>
			<span>2026</span>
			<span class="text-accent/40">|</span>
			<span>~30 min read</span>
		</div>
		<div class="mx-auto mt-12 h-px w-24 bg-accent/30"></div>
	</header>

	{/* Three-column body: TOC | Content | Sidenotes */}
	<div class="transformers-layout">
		{/* Left TOC sidebar — desktop only */}
		<nav id="toc-sidebar" class="toc-sidebar" aria-label="Table of contents">
			<div class="toc-inner">
				<ul class="toc-list">
					{
						sections.map((section) => (
							<li>
								<a
									href={`#${section.id}`}
									class="toc-dot-link"
									data-section={section.id}
									aria-label={section.title}
								>
									<span class="toc-dot"></span>
									<span class="toc-label">{section.number} {section.title}</span>
								</a>
							</li>
						))
					}
				</ul>
			</div>
		</nav>

		{/* Main content column */}
		<article class="transformers-content">

			{/* ===== Section 01: Preamble ===== */}
			<section id="preamble" class="mb-24 scroll-mt-20">
				<div class="mb-8 flex items-baseline gap-4">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-accent/60">01</span>
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent-2 sm:text-3xl">Preamble</h2>
						<p class="mt-1 text-base text-textColor/60">Why transformers changed everything</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				{/* Introductory prose */}
				<div class="preamble-prose">
					<p>
						In 2017, a team at Google published a paper with an unassuming title:
						<em>"Attention Is All You Need."</em> It introduced the <strong>transformer</strong> — an
						architecture that would go on to power GPT, BERT, Claude, Stable Diffusion,
						AlphaFold, and nearly every major AI breakthrough since.
					</p>
					<p>
						Before transformers, sequence models processed tokens one at a time.
						Recurrent neural networks (RNNs) and their variants — LSTMs and GRUs —
						passed a hidden state forward step by step, like reading a book one word
						at a time and trying to remember the whole plot through a single bottleneck of memory.
					</p>
					<p>
						This sequential constraint meant two things: training was slow (you couldn't
						parallelize), and long-range dependencies were hard to capture. By the time
						the model reached the end of a long paragraph, the beginning had faded.
					</p>
					<p>
						The transformer's key insight was radical: <strong>throw away recurrence entirely.</strong>
						Instead, let every token attend to every other token simultaneously. This
						mechanism — <em>self-attention</em> — lets the model look at the entire input at
						once, deciding which parts matter for each part.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Timeline: RNNs → LSTMs → Attention → Transformers */}
				<div class="preamble-timeline" aria-label="Evolution from RNNs to Transformers">
					<div class="timeline-track"></div>
					<div class="timeline-item">
						<div class="timeline-dot timeline-dot-past"></div>
						<div class="timeline-content">
							<span class="timeline-year">~2014</span>
							<span class="timeline-label">RNNs &amp; LSTMs</span>
							<span class="timeline-desc">Sequential processing, one token at a time</span>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="timeline-item">
						<div class="timeline-dot timeline-dot-past"></div>
						<div class="timeline-content">
							<span class="timeline-year">2015</span>
							<span class="timeline-label">Attention (Bahdanau)</span>
							<span class="timeline-desc">Learned to focus on relevant input positions</span>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="timeline-item">
						<div class="timeline-dot timeline-dot-present"></div>
						<div class="timeline-content">
							<span class="timeline-year">2017</span>
							<span class="timeline-label">Transformer</span>
							<span class="timeline-desc">Attention is all you need — no recurrence</span>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="timeline-item">
						<div class="timeline-dot timeline-dot-future"></div>
						<div class="timeline-content">
							<span class="timeline-year">2018+</span>
							<span class="timeline-label">GPT, BERT, and beyond</span>
							<span class="timeline-desc">Scaling transforms everything</span>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				{/* Visual vocabulary — component color legend */}
				<div class="preamble-vocab">
					<p class="vocab-intro">
						Throughout this explainer, we'll use a consistent visual language.
						Each major component has its own color, so you'll always know what you're looking at:
					</p>
					<div class="vocab-grid">
						<div class="vocab-item">
							<div class="vocab-swatch vocab-embedding"></div>
							<div class="vocab-meta">
								<span class="vocab-name">Embedding</span>
								<span class="vocab-hint">Words &rarr; vectors</span>
							</div>
						</div>
						<div class="vocab-item">
							<div class="vocab-swatch vocab-attention"></div>
							<div class="vocab-meta">
								<span class="vocab-name">Attention</span>
								<span class="vocab-hint">Tokens talk to each other</span>
							</div>
						</div>
						<div class="vocab-item">
							<div class="vocab-swatch vocab-ffn"></div>
							<div class="vocab-meta">
								<span class="vocab-name">Feed-Forward</span>
								<span class="vocab-hint">Per-token computation</span>
							</div>
						</div>
						<div class="vocab-item">
							<div class="vocab-swatch vocab-residual"></div>
							<div class="vocab-meta">
								<span class="vocab-name">Residual Stream</span>
								<span class="vocab-hint">The information highway</span>
							</div>
						</div>
						<div class="vocab-item">
							<div class="vocab-swatch vocab-output"></div>
							<div class="vocab-meta">
								<span class="vocab-name">Output</span>
								<span class="vocab-hint">Vectors &rarr; words</span>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				{/* Black box teaser */}
				<div class="preamble-teaser">
					<div class="teaser-box">
						<div class="teaser-label-in">tokens in</div>
						<div class="teaser-blackbox">
							<span class="teaser-q">?</span>
						</div>
						<div class="teaser-label-out">prediction out</div>
					</div>
					<p class="teaser-text">
						Right now, the transformer is a black box. Tokens go in, a prediction comes out.
						Over the next eight sections, we'll open this box — layer by layer, mechanism
						by mechanism — until there's nothing left to hide.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			{/* ===== Section 02: Architecture Overview ===== */}
			<section id="architecture" class="mb-24 scroll-mt-20">
				<div class="mb-8 flex items-baseline gap-4">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-accent/60">02</span>
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent-2 sm:text-3xl">Architecture Overview</h2>
						<p class="mt-1 text-base text-textColor/60">The full picture, from input to output</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="arch-prose">
					<p>
						Here's the entire transformer, laid out as a pipeline. Data flows upward: raw text enters
						at the bottom, and a prediction emerges at the top. Each block is something we'll explore
						in depth — think of this diagram as the <strong>map</strong> for the rest of the page.
					</p>
					<p>
						Hover over any block to learn what it does. When you're ready, press
						<strong>Animate</strong> to watch data flow through the full architecture.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Interactive block diagram */}
				<div class="arch-diagram-wrap">
					<div class="arch-controls">
						<button id="arch-animate-btn" class="arch-animate-btn">
							<svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none"><polygon points="3,1 12,7 3,13" fill="currentColor"/></svg>
							Animate
						</button>
					</div>

					<div class="arch-diagram" id="arch-diagram">
						{/* Output / Softmax */}
						<div class="arch-block arch-output" data-arch-id="output" data-tip="Converts the final vector into a probability distribution over the vocabulary — picking the next token.">
							<span class="arch-block-label">Softmax Output</span>
						</div>

						{/* Unembedding */}
						<div class="arch-block arch-output" data-arch-id="unembed" data-tip="Projects the residual stream vector back to vocabulary size via the unembedding matrix — the reverse of token embedding.">
							<span class="arch-block-label">Unembedding</span>
						</div>

						<div class="arch-arrow">&#8593;</div>

						{/* Layer norm (final) */}
						<div class="arch-block arch-norm" data-arch-id="final-norm" data-tip="A final layer normalization that stabilizes the output before unembedding.">
							<span class="arch-block-label">Layer Norm</span>
						</div>

						{/* Transformer layer (repeated) */}
						<div class="arch-layer-group">
							<div class="arch-layer-bracket">&times;N layers</div>

							{/* Residual + FFN */}
							<div class="arch-residual-wrap">
								<div class="arch-residual-line" data-tip="The residual connection adds the FFN output back to the stream, preserving earlier information."></div>
								<div class="arch-block arch-ffn" data-arch-id="ffn" data-tip="A two-layer neural network (expand → activate → compress) applied independently to each token. Acts as a learned lookup table.">
									<span class="arch-block-label">Feed-Forward Network</span>
								</div>
								<div class="arch-block arch-norm arch-norm-sm" data-arch-id="norm2" data-tip="Layer normalization before the feed-forward network.">
									<span class="arch-block-label">Layer Norm</span>
								</div>
							</div>

							<div class="arch-add-label">+ residual</div>

							{/* Residual + Attention */}
							<div class="arch-residual-wrap">
								<div class="arch-residual-line" data-tip="The residual connection adds the attention output back to the stream — this is what makes deep transformers trainable."></div>
								<div class="arch-block arch-attention" data-arch-id="attention" data-tip="Multi-head self-attention: every token computes queries, keys, and values, then attends to every other token to gather context.">
									<span class="arch-block-label">Multi-Head Attention</span>
								</div>
								<div class="arch-block arch-norm arch-norm-sm" data-arch-id="norm1" data-tip="Layer normalization before the attention mechanism.">
									<span class="arch-block-label">Layer Norm</span>
								</div>
							</div>

							<div class="arch-add-label">+ residual</div>
						</div>

						<div class="arch-arrow">&#8593;</div>

						{/* Positional encoding */}
						<div class="arch-block arch-embedding" data-arch-id="pos-enc" data-tip="Adds position information (sine/cosine waves) to each embedding, so the model knows word order.">
							<span class="arch-block-label">+ Positional Encoding</span>
						</div>

						{/* Token embedding */}
						<div class="arch-block arch-embedding" data-arch-id="embed" data-tip="Looks up each token in a learned embedding table, converting it from a discrete ID into a dense vector.">
							<span class="arch-block-label">Token Embedding</span>
						</div>

						<div class="arch-arrow">&#8593;</div>

						{/* Input */}
						<div class="arch-input-label">Input tokens</div>
					</div>

					{/* Tooltip */}
					<div class="arch-tooltip" id="arch-tooltip" aria-hidden="true"></div>
				</div>

				<p class="arch-outro">
					In the sections that follow, we'll zoom into each of these blocks — starting
					with how raw text becomes vectors (embeddings), then diving into the attention
					mechanism, feed-forward networks, and the residual stream that ties it all together.
				</p>
			</section>

			{/* ===== Section 03: Embeddings — Tokenization ===== */}
			<section id="embeddings" class="mb-24 scroll-mt-20">
				<div class="mb-8 flex items-baseline gap-4">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-accent/60">03</span>
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent-2 sm:text-3xl">Embeddings</h2>
						<p class="mt-1 text-base text-textColor/60">Turning words into numbers</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				{/* Linkage callout */}
				<div class="linkage-callout linkage-embedding">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>We're now zooming into the <strong>Token Embedding</strong> block at the bottom of the architecture diagram.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Before a transformer can do anything, it needs to convert raw text into numbers.
						This happens in two steps: first, the text is split into <strong>tokens</strong> —
						small pieces that the model treats as its atomic units — and then each token is
						looked up in a table to produce a dense vector.
					</p>
					<p>
						Modern models don't tokenize by whole words. Instead, they use <strong>subword
						tokenization</strong> (like BPE — Byte Pair Encoding). Common words stay whole,
						but rare or long words get broken into smaller pieces. This lets the model handle
						any text — even words it's never seen — with a fixed-size vocabulary
						(typically 32k–100k tokens).
					</p>
					<p>
						Try typing below to see how text gets split into tokens. Notice how common words
						often stay intact, while rarer words get broken into subword pieces.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Interactive tokenizer widget */}
				<div class="tokenizer-widget">
					<label class="tokenizer-label" for="tokenizer-input">Type some text:</label>
					<textarea
						id="tokenizer-input"
						class="tokenizer-input"
						rows="2"
						placeholder="The cat sat on the mat."
						spellcheck="false"
					></textarea>
					<div class="tokenizer-output-wrap">
						<div class="tokenizer-output-label">
							Tokens <span class="tokenizer-count" id="tokenizer-count"></span>
						</div>
						<div class="tokenizer-output" id="tokenizer-output">
							<span class="tokenizer-placeholder">Type above to see tokens&hellip;</span>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="tokenizer-legend">
						<span class="tokenizer-legend-item"><span class="token-chip token-whole-example">&nbsp;</span> Whole word</span>
						<span class="tokenizer-legend-item"><span class="token-chip token-sub-example">&nbsp;</span> Subword piece</span>
						<span class="tokenizer-legend-item"><span class="token-chip token-special-example">&nbsp;</span> Punctuation / special</span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Each token maps to a unique integer ID — its index in the vocabulary table.
						The embedding layer then looks up this ID in a learned matrix of shape
						<code>[vocab_size &times; d_model]</code>, producing a dense vector (typically
						768 or 1024 dimensions) that encodes the token's meaning.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Token → Vector transition */}
				<div class="embed-transition">
					<div class="embed-transition-label">Each token becomes a dense vector:</div>
					<div class="embed-transition-rows">
						<div class="embed-transition-row">
							<span class="token-chip token-whole">cat</span>
							<span class="embed-arrow">&rarr;</span>
							<span class="embed-vector">[0.23, &minus;0.41, 0.87, 0.05, &hellip;]</span>
						</div>
						<div class="embed-transition-row">
							<span class="token-chip token-whole">dog</span>
							<span class="embed-arrow">&rarr;</span>
							<span class="embed-vector">[0.21, &minus;0.38, 0.91, 0.02, &hellip;]</span>
						</div>
						<div class="embed-transition-row">
							<span class="token-chip token-whole">king</span>
							<span class="embed-arrow">&rarr;</span>
							<span class="embed-vector">[&minus;0.74, 0.62, &minus;0.15, 0.88, &hellip;]</span>
						</div>
					</div>
					<p class="embed-transition-note">
						Notice that <strong>cat</strong> and <strong>dog</strong> have similar vectors — they're both animals.
						<strong>King</strong> points in a completely different direction. When we project these high-dimensional
						vectors down to 2D, similar words cluster together:
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* 2D Embedding scatter plot */}
				<div class="embed-scatter-wrap">
					<div class="embed-scatter-header">
						<span class="embed-scatter-title">Word embeddings projected to 2D</span>
						<span class="embed-scatter-subtitle">hover over any point to see the word</span>
					</div>
					<svg id="embed-scatter" class="embed-scatter" viewBox="0 0 600 420" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet">
						{/* Grid lines */}
						<line x1="60" y1="380" x2="580" y2="380" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1"/>
						<line x1="60" y1="300" x2="580" y2="300" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.05" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
						<line x1="60" y1="220" x2="580" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.05" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
						<line x1="60" y1="140" x2="580" y2="140" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.05" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
						<line x1="60" y1="60" x2="580" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.05" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
						<line x1="60" y1="60" x2="60" y2="380" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1"/>
						{/* Cluster backgrounds — subtle ovals (pointer-events: none so dots are hoverable) */}
						<ellipse cx="140" cy="310" rx="80" ry="55" fill="hsl(210 70% 55%)" fill-opacity="0.04" stroke="hsl(210 70% 55%)" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" pointer-events="none"/>
						<ellipse cx="460" cy="110" rx="85" ry="55" fill="hsl(35 90% 55%)" fill-opacity="0.04" stroke="hsl(35 90% 55%)" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" pointer-events="none"/>
						<ellipse cx="150" cy="120" rx="75" ry="55" fill="hsl(350 65% 55%)" fill-opacity="0.04" stroke="hsl(350 65% 55%)" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" pointer-events="none"/>
						<ellipse cx="440" cy="310" rx="80" ry="55" fill="hsl(150 55% 45%)" fill-opacity="0.04" stroke="hsl(150 55% 45%)" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" pointer-events="none"/>
						<ellipse cx="300" cy="210" rx="70" ry="50" fill="hsl(270 50% 58%)" fill-opacity="0.04" stroke="hsl(270 50% 58%)" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" pointer-events="none"/>
						{/* Data points will be rendered by JS for interactivity */}
					</svg>
					<div class="embed-scatter-legend" id="embed-scatter-legend"></div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						The key insight is that embedding vectors aren't random — <strong>meaning is
						encoded in geometry.</strong> Words that are used in similar contexts end up
						with similar vectors, forming clusters in the high-dimensional space. The model
						learns these representations during training, and they capture surprisingly rich
						semantic relationships.
					</p>
					<p>
						But there's a problem: these vectors know <em>what</em> each token means, but they
						don't know <em>where</em> each token is. The word "bank" has the same embedding whether
						it's the first or last word in a sentence. The transformer needs position information too.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* ===== Positional Encoding subsection ===== */}
				<div class="subsection-divider"></div>

				<h3 class="subsection-title">
					<span class="subsection-label">03b</span>
					Positional Encoding
				</h3>

				{/* Linkage callout */}
				<div class="linkage-callout linkage-embedding">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>We're now at the <strong>+ Positional Encoding</strong> block — the second step in the input pipeline, right after token embedding.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Self-attention is <strong>permutation-invariant</strong> — it treats all tokens
						the same regardless of order. "The cat sat on the mat" and "mat the on sat cat the"
						would produce identical attention patterns without positional information. That's
						a problem, because word order carries meaning.
					</p>
					<p>
						The solution: <strong>add</strong> a unique positional signal to each embedding vector.
						The original transformer uses sine and cosine waves at different frequencies — each
						position gets a unique "fingerprint" that the model can learn to interpret.
					</p>
					<p>
						The formula is elegant: for position <em>pos</em> and dimension <em>i</em>:
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="posenc-formula">
					<div class="posenc-formula-row">
						<span class="posenc-fn">PE</span><sub>(pos, 2i)</sub> = sin(pos / 10000<sup>2i/d</sup>)
					</div>
					<div class="posenc-formula-row">
						<span class="posenc-fn">PE</span><sub>(pos, 2i+1)</sub> = cos(pos / 10000<sup>2i/d</sup>)
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Low dimensions oscillate rapidly (high frequency), capturing fine-grained position.
						Higher dimensions oscillate slowly (low frequency), capturing broad position. Together,
						they give each position a unique signature. Drag the slider below to see how the
						encoding vector changes at each position:
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Interactive position slider + wave visualization */}
				<div class="posenc-interactive">
					<div class="posenc-slider-wrap">
						<label class="posenc-slider-label" for="posenc-slider">
							Position: <span id="posenc-pos-value" class="posenc-pos-value">0</span>
						</label>
						<input type="range" id="posenc-slider" class="posenc-slider" min="0" max="31" value="0" step="1"/>
						<div class="posenc-slider-range">
							<span>0</span>
							<span>31</span>
						</div>
					</div>

					{/* Wave visualization — SVG canvas */}
					<div class="posenc-waves-wrap">
						<div class="posenc-waves-label">Encoding vector at position <span id="posenc-wave-pos">0</span></div>
						<svg id="posenc-waves" class="posenc-waves" viewBox="0 0 600 160" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet">
							{/* Bars rendered by JS */}
							<line x1="30" y1="80" x2="580" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.1" stroke-width="1"/>
							<text x="12" y="14" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.2" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle">+1</text>
							<text x="12" y="84" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.2" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle">0</text>
							<text x="12" y="156" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.2" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle">-1</text>
						</svg>
					</div>
				</div>

				{/* Heatmap of positional encoding matrix */}
				<div class="posenc-heatmap-wrap">
					<div class="posenc-heatmap-label">Positional encoding matrix (32 positions &times; 32 dimensions)</div>
					<canvas id="posenc-heatmap" class="posenc-heatmap" width="480" height="240"></canvas>
					<div class="posenc-heatmap-axes">
						<span class="posenc-axis-label">Dimension &rarr;</span>
						<span class="posenc-axis-label-y">Position &darr;</span>
					</div>
					<div class="posenc-heatmap-scale">
						<span class="posenc-scale-label">&minus;1</span>
						<div class="posenc-scale-bar"></div>
						<span class="posenc-scale-label">+1</span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose">
					<p>
						Each row in the heatmap is a position's encoding — its unique fingerprint.
						Notice the striped pattern: low-numbered dimensions (left) oscillate rapidly
						across positions, while higher dimensions (right) change slowly. No two rows
						are identical, so the model can always tell positions apart.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* LINKAGE: embedding + position = input to attention */}
				<div class="posenc-handoff">
					<div class="posenc-handoff-label">The input to attention:</div>
					<div class="posenc-handoff-row">
						<div class="posenc-handoff-box posenc-handoff-embed">
							<span class="posenc-handoff-icon">E</span>
							<span>Token Embedding</span>
						</div>
						<span class="posenc-handoff-plus">+</span>
						<div class="posenc-handoff-box posenc-handoff-pos">
							<span class="posenc-handoff-icon">P</span>
							<span>Position Encoding</span>
						</div>
						<span class="posenc-handoff-eq">=</span>
						<div class="posenc-handoff-box posenc-handoff-result">
							<span class="posenc-handoff-icon">&rarr;</span>
							<span>Input Vector</span>
						</div>
					</div>
					<p class="posenc-handoff-note">
						Each token now carries both its <strong>meaning</strong> (from the embedding) and its
						<strong>position</strong> (from the positional encoding). These combined vectors are what
						flow into the attention mechanism — the subject of the next section.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			{/* ===== Sections 04–09: Placeholders ===== */}
			{
				sections.filter(s => !['preamble','architecture','embeddings'].includes(s.id)).map((section) => (
					<section
						id={section.id}
						class="section-placeholder mb-24 scroll-mt-20"
